<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In 1974 in the seminal paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> George Bergman introduced machinery whereby by adding sufficient generators and relations to an algebra, one can lift any pair of non-zero finitely generated projective modules to become isomorphic. To be precise, let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be an algebra over a field <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> be pairs of non-zero finitely generated projective left <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Bergman’s machinery adjoins certain generators and relations to <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the resulted extension algebra <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> obtained provides universal isomorphisms of modules, <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}P_{i}\cong S\otimes_{R}Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}P_{i}\cong S\otimes_{R}Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, the non-stable <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of the algebra <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained from that of <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by identifying the pairs <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> in the structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Starting from a field <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and choosing the pair of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{k}^{n},\mathsf{k}^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{k}^{n},\mathsf{k}^{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m\in\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m\in\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, Bergman observed that his machinery retrieves the celebrated
Leavitt algebra <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{n}\cong\mathsf{k}^{n}\otimes_{\mathsf{k}}R\cong\mathsf{k}^{m}\otimes_{\mathsf{k}}R\cong R^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{n}\cong\mathsf{k}^{n}\otimes_{\mathsf{k}}R\cong\mathsf{k}^{m}\otimes_{\mathsf{k}}R\cong R^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Many combinatorial algebras constructed in the last 50 years, such as Leavitt path algebras and their generalisations, can be obtained from Bergman’s machinery.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Two natural questions immediately arise:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The structural properties</span>: To describe the structure of the extension algebra <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the pairs <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The classification</span>: Given pairs <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P^{\prime}_{i},Q^{\prime}_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P^{\prime}_{i},Q^{\prime}_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, for <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, to describe how the extension <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the Bergman machinery, respectively, compare.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We highlighted Leavitt path algebras as substantial activity is currently taking place
on both questions (1) and (2).
In fact, there are several conjectures concering the classification problems and what could be the right invariant for Leavitt path algebras <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Leavitt path algebras associated to graphs have rich <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded structures (arising by assigning degree <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to edges) which play a vital role in their studies. Their non-stable <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded</em> <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory is related to invariants of symbolic dynamics and the equivariant <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of operator algebras and the current open conjecture is that the graded <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory could be a complete invariant for these algebras (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and §<a href="#S9" title="9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). This is one of the main motivations of this project: to study the Bergman machinery in the graded setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative ring and let <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a unital <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Further let <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a finitely generated projective <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Let <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category of <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math>. Bergman’s work started by showing that the following functor is representable:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{F}_{N,P}:R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&amp;\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\
T&amp;\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}(T\otimes_{R}N,T\otimes_{R}P).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>T</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟼</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{F}_{N,P}:R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&amp;\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\
T&amp;\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}(T\otimes_{R}N,T\otimes_{R}P).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">That is, there exists a universal algebra <math id="S1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (or the initial object <math id="S1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math> in an appropriate category), denoted by <math id="S1.p6.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f:\overline{N}\rightarrow\overline{P}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f:\overline{N}\rightarrow\overline{P}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides the <math id="S1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism <math id="S1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}N\rightarrow S\otimes_{R}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}N\rightarrow S\otimes_{R}P</annotation></semantics></math>. Bergman further showed that there exists a universal algebra <math id="S1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the extension of a given <math id="S1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphim <math id="S1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:N\rightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:N\rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Combining these two constructions, allows for forming very interesting universal rings. For example, given a homomorphism <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:P\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:P\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math>, one can construct a universal ring <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{f}^{-1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{f}^{-1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where the extension of <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> becomes invertible. The two important universal constructions needed to realise any conical monoid are the following: Let <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be non-zero finitely generated projective left <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Constructing the universal homomorphisms <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}:\overline{\overline{Q}}\rightarrow\overline{\overline{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}:\overline{\overline{Q}}\rightarrow\overline{\overline{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> in subsequent extensions and setting <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}\overline{h}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}\overline{h}-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{h}h^{\prime}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{h}h^{\prime}-1</annotation></semantics></math> to zero, we obtain the universal ring</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S:=R\big\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:=R\big\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, for <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a non-zero finitely generated projective left <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module, by constructing the universal homomorphism <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:P\rightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:P\rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> and setting the homomorphism <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2}-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2}-e</annotation></semantics></math> to zero we form a universal ring</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T:=R\big\langle e:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~e^{2}=e\big\rangle," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.497em">;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=R\big\langle e:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~e^{2}=e\big\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">such that the extension of <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an idempotent endomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Bergman then proved <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorems 5.1, 5.2]</cite> that <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(S)\cong\mathcal{V}(R)/\langle[P]=[Q]\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(S)\cong\mathcal{V}(R)/\langle[P]=[Q]\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(T)\cong\langle\mathcal{V}(R),[P_{1}],[P_{2}]\rangle/\langle[P]=[P_{1}]+[P_{2}]\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(T)\cong\langle\mathcal{V}(R),[P_{1}],[P_{2}]\rangle/\langle[P]=[P_{1}]+[P_{2}]\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, for the universal rings <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>), respectively. Here <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is the monoid of isomorphism classes of finitely generated projective left <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.
These constructions allowed Bergman to build up a machinery to realise any (finitely generated) conical commutative monoid as the non-stable <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of a ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Graded algebras frequently appear when there is a group acting on an algebraic structure. A graded ring can be a model of an algebraic structure that captures and reflects the time evolution and the dynamics.
A ring <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded</em> by a group <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> when, roughly, <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned by <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in a way that is compatible with the structure of <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. (See §<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Graded algebras ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> for more details.) Consequently, three categories play a prominent role in this setting: the category of left <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the category of graded left <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the category of left <math id="S1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subring of <math id="S1.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of the elements in the partition corresponding to the identity element <math id="S1.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. A substantial portion of the theory of graded rings concerns the relationships between these categories. While the applications of this theory are numerous, one prominent example is the fundamental theorem of <math id="S1.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory, proved by Quillen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, using the category of graded modules in a crucial way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Our starting point is to consider a <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> over a <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative ring <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded (left) <math id="S1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded finitely generated projective (left) <math id="S1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian group, and consider the corresponding functors in the graded setting:</p>
