</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-gr-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the category of <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a graded <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper we show that <math id="S1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable, i.e., there is a <math id="S1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded algebra <math id="S1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}(S,-)\cong\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>gr</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}(S,-)\cong\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly graded, then <math id="S1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cong R\ast_{R_{0}}S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cong R\ast_{R_{0}}S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the representation for <math id="S1.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{N_{0},P_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{N_{0},P_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math> is the coproduct. Forgetting the grading on <math id="S1.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> gives us the representation for
<math id="S1.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{N,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{N,P}</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Having these graded universal constructions, we can realise any conical commutative <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid as the nonstable graded <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of a suitable graded ring. Namely, for such a <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="S1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids. Here <math id="S1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is the monoid of isomorphism classes of graded finitely generated projective <math id="S1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and the action of <math id="S1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is by shift of degrees (Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 8.1. ‣ 8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>).
In order to carry out our <math id="S1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid realisations, we had two options; either to develop the entire theory of monoids for co-products rings (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>) in the graded setting, or to start with a graded ring, and use the fact that the category of graded modules is equivalent to the category of modules over its corresponding smash product (i.e., to pass from the graded setting to the non-graded case and use the available techniques).
We opted for the second option; we pass from the graded structure via smash product to the category of modules and
use the established results in the non-graded setting. The price we had to pay for such a shortcut,
was to drop some generality, such as working with graded <math id="S1.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras, where <math id="S1.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a field concentrated in degree zero, rather than with <math id="S1.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras, where <math id="S1.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Our realisations of <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids allow us to provide a short proof to one of the Graded Classification Conjectures (See Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 9.1. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>(1)): Let <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra where <math id="S1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is concentrated in degree zero and <math id="S1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is cancellative. Then any order preserving <math id="S1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-module
homomorphism <math id="S1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=[A]</annotation></semantics></math>
is induced by a unital <math id="S1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism <math id="S1.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S1.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S1.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Leavitt path algebra associated to the graph <math id="S1.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Replacing the algebra <math id="S1.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with a Leavitt path algebra <math id="S1.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> provides a positive answer to the fullness conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">In general given two finitely generated projective modules, it is not straightforward to describe Bergman’s (localisation) algebras explicitly. Given finitely generated <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, and a map <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:P\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:P\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math>, there are few instances where the localisation rings <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{f}^{-1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{f}^{-1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> are explicitly described. We refer to the paper of Sheiham <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> where the localisation of triangular matrix rings has been described. In this paper, by representing the finitely generated projective modules by the corresponding idempotent matrices, we first describe the Bergman’s constructions in terms of generators and relations. Thus given idempotent matrices <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the Bergman algebra <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> via generators and relations such that <math id="S1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> become equivalent idempotents in <math id="S1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> in a universal way.
The construction can be extended to families of pairs of idempotents <math id="S1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact this construction will be carried out for graded rings and homogeneous idempotents in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The algebra <math id="S1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained this way, coincides with
the universal localisation ring <math id="S1.p16.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f,f^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\mid i\in I\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f,f^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\mid i\in I\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, for pairs of finitely generated projective modules <math id="S1.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i}),i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i}),i\in I</annotation></semantics></math> which represent <math id="S1.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{i},f_{i}),{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{i},f_{i}),{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Returning to the graded setting, we show that when <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-semisimple ring which is concentrated in degree zero, then the universal localisation ring <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f,f^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\mid i\in I\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f,f^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\mid i\in I\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, for any family of pairs of finitely generated projective modules <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i}),i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i}),i\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, is graded isomorphic to a Leavitt path algebra of a hypergraph and conversely any such hyper Leavitt path algebra can be realised as a graded Bergman’s construction over a <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative <math id="S1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-semisimple ring (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 4.10. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>). Built on this, we show that any finitely generated conical <math id="S1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid can be realised as the non-stable graded <math id="S1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of a hyper Leavitt path algebra (Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 8.2. ‣ 8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned, starting with a field <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and projective modules <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, Bergman’s machinery would give the Leavitt algebra <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. This ring can be graded by assigning <math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> to the suitable generators. However, we can start with a field <math id="S1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> concentrated in degree zero, and consider the <math id="S1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded projective modules <math id="S1.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}(-1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}(-1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the projective module <math id="S1.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> shifted by <math id="S1.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying our graded Bergman construction, we obtain the algebra <math id="S1.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n)</annotation></semantics></math> which is naturally <math id="S1.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded from the outset. This allows us to build the graded invariants such as non-stable graded <math id="S1.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory from the machinery we develop.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organised as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a background needed for the concepts of <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids and graded algebras, as well as the notion of smash products. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we recall the notion of Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs, which generalises several extensions of Leavitt path algebras. This is the generalisation which covers all Bergman algebras constructed from commutative semisimple rings as a base ring. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we introduce Bergman algebras, via generators and relations, both the non-graded version and the graded version. Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> which is the core part of this paper, carries out Bergman’s localisation construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> in the setting of graded rings.
Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Smash products of graded Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> investigates how the graded Bergman localisation behaves under the smash product. This is needed in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> to compute the non-stable graded <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of graded Bergman algebras. Section <a href="#S8" title="8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> develops many consequences of the graded Bergman machinery: we realise any conical <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid as the non-stable graded <math id="S1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of an appropriate graded algebra. In fact we show that any finitely generated <math id="S1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid can be obtained as the non-stable graded <math id="S1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of a hyper Leavitt path algebra. Using our result, we provide a short positive answer to one of the Graded Classification Conjectures (Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 9.1. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>(1)) for Leavitt path algebras in Section <a href="#S9" title="9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, all rings are unital and all modules are considered as left modules unless otherwise stated. <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the natural numbers with <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}^{+}=\mathbb{N}\backslash\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}^{+}=\mathbb{N}\backslash\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span><math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids and graded algebras</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span><math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monoid</span> is a semigroup with an identity element. Throughout this paper monoids are commutative, written additively, with the identity element denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. A monoid homomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:M\rightarrow N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:M\rightarrow N</annotation></semantics></math> is a map between monoids <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> which respects the structures and satisfies <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Every monoid <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with a natural preordering: <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+p=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+p=m</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We write</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\propto m\text{ if }n\leq km,\text{ for some }k\in\mathbb{N}^{+}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\propto m\text{ if }n\leq km,\text{ for some }k\in\mathbb{N}^{+},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\asymp m\text{ if }n\propto m\text{ and }m\propto n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\asymp m\text{ if }n\propto m\text{ and }m\propto n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\asymp m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≍</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\asymp m</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq kn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq kn</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq km" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq km</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in M</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">an order unit</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\propto i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∝</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\propto i</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conical</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+n=0</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. We say <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.p1.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cancellative</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}+n=m_{2}+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}+n=m_{2}+n</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}=m_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}=m_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},m_{2},n\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},m_{2},n\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a group <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid</em> consists of a monoid <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with an action of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (by monoid automorphisms). We denote the action of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\alpha}m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>m</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\alpha}m</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout this paper <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> will be an abelian group and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> a commutative monoid. A monoid homomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon M\rightarrow N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon M\rightarrow N</annotation></semantics></math> between two <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids is called <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monoid homomorphism</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> respects the actions of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi({}^{\alpha}m)={}^{\alpha}\phi(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>m</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>ϕ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi({}^{\alpha}m)={}^{\alpha}\phi(m)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. The natural preordering is respected by the action of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">order unit</span> of a <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is an element <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in M</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, there are <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{t}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{t}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq\sum_{k=1}^{t}{}^{\gamma_{k}}i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq\sum_{k=1}^{t}{}^{\gamma_{k}}i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> along with the order unit <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,i)</annotation></semantics></math> and call it a <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pointed <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid</em>. A morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(M,i)\rightarrow(N,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(M,i)\rightarrow(N,j)</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of pointed <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids, is a <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)=j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The order unit <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong order unit</em> if for any <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, we can choose all <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. Γ-monoids ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We say <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invariant order unit</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\gamma}i=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\gamma}i=i</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">congruence</span> on a <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalence relation <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\sim m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sim m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sim n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sim n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+n\sim m^{\prime}+n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+n\sim m^{\prime}+n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\sim m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sim m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\gamma}m\sim{}^{\gamma}m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>m</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>∼</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>m</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\gamma}m\sim{}^{\gamma}m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,m^{\prime},n,n^{\prime}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,m^{\prime},n,n^{\prime}\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is a congruence on <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M/\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid in the obvious way. We call <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M/\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/\sim</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quotient</span> of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{i},n_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{i},n_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, a family of pairs of elements of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, then <em id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the congruence on the <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the relations <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></em> will be the congruence generated on <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a monoid by the relations <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{\gamma}m_{i}={}^{\gamma}n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>m</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>n</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>i</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\gamma}m_{i}={}^{\gamma}n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The action of the abelian group <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> respects the relations <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\propto</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\asymp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≍</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\asymp</annotation></semantics></math> and one can see that <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\asymp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≍</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\asymp</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalence relation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in M</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-order unit. Then the following are equivalent.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The element </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a strong order unit;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> we have </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\asymp{}^{\gamma}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≍</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>i</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\asymp{}^{\gamma}i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The equivalence class </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[i]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> under </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\asymp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≍</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\asymp</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, which is clearly a
sub-semigroup of </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, is in fact a </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-subsemigroup of </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a strong order-unit, then all
such elements form an equivalence class under </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\asymp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≍</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\asymp</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proofs are straightforward.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly the image of a homomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\to N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\to N</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}f</annotation></semantics></math>, is a <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-submonoid of <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">kernel</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker f</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the congruence <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\sim m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sim m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(m)=f(m^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(m)=f(m^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/\ker f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/\ker f</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid and the canonical homomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/\ker f\rightarrow N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/\ker f\rightarrow N</annotation></semantics></math> is injective (see Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3 (Homomorphism theorem). ‣ 2.1. Γ-monoids ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a set. The <span id="S2.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">free <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid on <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span>, denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\langle X\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\langle X\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, is constructed as the free monoid on the set <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, with the action of <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\gamma+\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\gamma+\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. To be precise,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F\langle X\rangle=\Big\{\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)\mid n_{x,\gamma}\geq 0\text{, for any }x\in X\text{ and }\gamma\in\Gamma;\text{ almost all }n_{x,\gamma}\text{ are zero}\Big\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>, for any </mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>;</mo><mtext> almost all </mtext><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> are zero</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\langle X\rangle=\Big\{\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)\mid n_{x,\gamma}\geq 0\text{, for any }x\in X\text{ and }\gamma\in\Gamma;\text{ almost all }n_{x,\gamma}\text{ are zero}\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.2" class="ltx_p">which becomes a <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid with addition</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)+\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n^{\prime}_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)=\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}(n_{x,\gamma}+n^{\prime}_{x,\gamma})(x,\gamma)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)+\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n^{\prime}_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)=\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}(n_{x,\gamma}+n^{\prime}_{x,\gamma})(x,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-action</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{\delta}\Big(\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)\Big)=\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}x(\gamma+\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\delta}\Big(\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)\Big)=\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}x(\gamma+\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.4" class="ltx_p">One checks easily that <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\langle X\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\langle X\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> has the following universal property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a set, <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid and <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to M</annotation></semantics></math> a map. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:X\to F\langle X\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:X\to F\langle X\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> be the map defined by <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\to x(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\to x(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a unique homomorphism <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:F\langle X\rangle\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:F\langle X\rangle\to M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids such that <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=hg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=hg</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Homomorphism theorem)<span id="S2.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\to N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\to N</annotation></semantics></math> be a homomorphism of <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/\ker f\cong\operatorname{im}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/\ker f\cong\operatorname{im}f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define a map <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}:M/\ker f\to\operatorname{im}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}:M/\ker f\to\operatorname{im}f</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}([m])=f(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}([m])=f(m)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined and bijective. One checks easily that <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a homomorphism of <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid is isomorphic to a quotient of a free <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid. It follows from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Γ-monoids ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> that there is a surjective homomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:F\langle X\rangle\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:F\langle X\rangle\to M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids. Thus <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\langle X\rangle/\ker\gamma\cong\operatorname{im}\gamma=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\langle X\rangle/\ker\gamma\cong\operatorname{im}\gamma=M</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3 (Homomorphism theorem). ‣ 2.1. Γ-monoids ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a set and <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a set of relations <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i},n_{i}\in F\langle X\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i},n_{i}\in F\langle X\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\langle X\mid R\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\langle X\mid R\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M\cong F\langle X\rangle/\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\cong F\langle X\rangle/\sim</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is the congruence on <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\langle X\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\langle X\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we say that <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid presented by the generating set <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and the relations <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Graded algebras</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we collect basic facts that we need about graded rings and graded algebras. We refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> for the theory of graded rings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian group with identity denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. A ring <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly without unit)
is called a <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring</em> if <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that each <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is an additive subgroup of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\gamma}R_{\delta}\subseteq R_{\gamma+\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\gamma}R_{\delta}\subseteq R_{\gamma+\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The group <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is
called the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">homogeneous component</em> of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R.</annotation></semantics></math> When it is clear from context
that a ring <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is graded by the group <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,</annotation></semantics></math> we simply say that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded
ring</em>. We denote the set of all homogeneous elements of the graded ring <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A unital <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strongly graded</em> if <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}R_{\beta}=R_{\alpha+\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}R_{\beta}=R_{\alpha+\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and it is <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">crossed-product</em> if each component <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> contains an invertible element. Furthermore, we say <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded field</em> if it is commutative and all non-zero homogeneous elements are invertible. The notion of strongly graded, in particular, is an important case as it widely generalises the case of group rings, and it has been gradually established that many results on group rings can be extended to strongly graded rings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative ring and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a unital <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring. We say <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra</em> if it is equipped with a graded ring homomorphism <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathsf{k}\rightarrow Z(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathsf{k}\rightarrow Z(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is the centre of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that since <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is abelian, <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded ring. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a graded <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Then we say <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></em> if it is equipped with a graded <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the category of
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-gr-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to be trivial we obtain the category of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For a graded <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shifted</em> graded
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\alpha)=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}M(\alpha)_{\gamma}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\alpha)=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}M(\alpha)_{\gamma},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\alpha)_{\gamma}=M_{\alpha+\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\alpha)_{\gamma}=M_{\alpha+\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, as an ungraded module, <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is a copy of
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, but the grading is shifted by <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For a <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, the category of graded <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the category of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules by <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shift functor</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\alpha}:R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\longrightarrow R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}},\quad M\longmapsto M(\alpha)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟼</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\alpha}:R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\longrightarrow R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}},\quad M\longmapsto M(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">is an isomorphism with the property <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\alpha}\mathcal{T}_{\beta}=\mathcal{T}_{\alpha+\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\alpha}\mathcal{T}_{\beta}=\mathcal{T}_{\alpha+\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a strongly <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. For any <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify the <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\gamma}\otimes_{R_{0}}N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\gamma}\otimes_{R_{0}}N</annotation></semantics></math> with its image in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\otimes_{R_{0}}N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\otimes_{R_{0}}N</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-bimodules, <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\otimes_{R_{0}}N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\otimes_{R_{0}}N</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module, with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\otimes_{R_{0}}N=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}\otimes_{R_{0}}N." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\otimes_{R_{0}}N=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}\otimes_{R_{0}}N.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
