<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We identify graded finitely generated projective modules with homogeneous idempotent matrices of degree zero. This is taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Lemma 3.2.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the following statements.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any graded finitely generated projective module gives rise to a homogeneous idempotent matrix of degree zero.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any homogeneous idempotent matrix of degree zero gives rise to a graded finitely generated projective module.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (1), let <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a graded finitely generated projective <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Then
there is a graded module <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\oplus Q\cong R^{n}(\overline{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\oplus Q\cong R^{n}(\overline{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{n})\in\Gamma^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{n})\in\Gamma^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the homomorphism <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\operatorname{End}_{R}(R^{n}(\overline{\alpha}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\operatorname{End}_{R}(R^{n}(\overline{\alpha}))</annotation></semantics></math>
which sends <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to zero and acts as identity on <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is an idempotent and
graded homomorphism of degree zero. Thus <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\operatorname{End}_{R}(R^{n}(\overline{\alpha}))_{0}\cong\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\operatorname{End}_{R}(R^{n}(\overline{\alpha}))_{0}\cong\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (2), let <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a homogeneous idempotent matrix of degree zero, where <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-e\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-e\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{n}(\overline{\alpha})=R^{n}e(\overline{\alpha})\oplus R^{n}(1-e)(\overline{\alpha})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{n}(\overline{\alpha})=R^{n}e(\overline{\alpha})\oplus R^{n}(1-e)(\overline{\alpha}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">This shows that <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{n}e(\overline{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{n}e(\overline{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded finitely generated projective <math id="S2.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In the first part of the paper we assume <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a commutative graded ring. In the second part, for the realisation of conical <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids, we assume <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a (graded) field concentrated in degree zero.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Non-stable Graded <math id="S2.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> with identity and a graded finitely generated projective (left) <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the class of graded <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules graded isomorphic to <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the set</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\big\{\,[P]\mid P\text{ is a graded finitely generated projective R-module}\,\big\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is a graded finitely generated projective R-module</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\big\{\,[P]\mid P\text{ is a graded finitely generated projective R-module}\,\big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with direct sum as addition constitutes a <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid structure defined as follows: <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[P]+[Q]=[P\oplus Q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]+[Q]=[P\oplus Q]</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[P]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{\gamma}[P]=[P(\gamma)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\gamma}[P]=[P(\gamma)]</annotation></semantics></math>. The group completion of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S2.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded Grothendieck group</em> and is denoted by <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, which
as the above discussion shows is a <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[\Gamma]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[\Gamma]</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following the notion of order unit for a <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid in §<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Γ-monoids ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>,
an element <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[P]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is an order unit for <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> if for any <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Q]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Q]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Q]\leq\sum_{1\leq i\leq n}[P(\gamma_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Q]\leq\sum_{1\leq i\leq n}[P(\gamma_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since for any graded finitely generated projective module <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a graded module <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\oplus P\cong R(\gamma_{1})\oplus R(\gamma_{2})\oplus\cdots\oplus R(\gamma_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\oplus P\cong R(\gamma_{1})\oplus R(\gamma_{2})\oplus\cdots\oplus R(\gamma_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R]</annotation></semantics></math> is an order unit (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, §3]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following easy proposition shows that the order unit <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> can determine when <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongly graded or a crossed product ring (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorems 2.10.1 and 2.10.2]</cite>). This will be used in Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 8.1. ‣ 8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring with identity and <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> the monoid of graded finitely generated projective modules. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The ring </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is strongly graded if and only if </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an strong order unit.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The ring </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is crossed-product if and only if </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an invariant order unit.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">(1) The ring <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly graded if and only if, for any <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\alpha}}r_{i}s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\alpha}}r_{i}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}\in R_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}\in R_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\in R_{-\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\in R_{-\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is equivalent to having matrices <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{r}}=(r_{1},\dots,r_{n_{\alpha}})\in\mathbb{M}_{1\times n_{\alpha}}(R)[\overline{\alpha}][0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{r}}=(r_{1},\dots,r_{n_{\alpha}})\in\mathbb{M}_{1\times n_{\alpha}}(R)[\overline{\alpha}][0]</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{s}}=(s_{1},\dots,s_{n_{\alpha}})^{t}\in\mathbb{M}_{n_{\alpha}\times 1}(R)[0][\overline{\alpha}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{s}}=(s_{1},\dots,s_{n_{\alpha}})^{t}\in\mathbb{M}_{n_{\alpha}\times 1}(R)[0][\overline{\alpha}]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{r}}{\mathbf{s}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{r}}{\mathbf{s}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In turn this is equivalent to having an epimorphism <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{n_{\alpha}}R\rightarrow R(\alpha)\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{n_{\alpha}}R\rightarrow R(\alpha)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R(\alpha)]\leq n_{\alpha}[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R(\alpha)]\leq n_{\alpha}[R]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is now equivalent to <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R]</annotation></semantics></math> being a strong order unit.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">(2) This follows from the observation that there is a graded <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphism <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\cong R(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\cong R(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there is an invertible element in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Graded hereditary rings</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a unital ring <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is called a left hereditary ring if any left ideal of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, Chapter 4]</cite>. Analogously, we say a <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="S2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left graded hereditary ring</em> if any graded left ideal of <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded projective module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition is the graded version of well-known equivalent properties of hereditary rings whose proof follows mutatis mutandis, with an attention given to the grading.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring. The following statements are equivalent.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is left graded hereditary;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any graded submodule of a graded projective left </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module is graded projective;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any graded submodule of a graded projective left </span><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module is projective;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graded left global dimension is less than two;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ext}_{R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}^{2}(-,-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ext</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ext}_{R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}^{2}(-,-)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> vanishes.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2.5. Graded hereditary rings ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>(3) implies that a <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring which is hereditary is indeed graded hereditary. However, the following example shows that a graded hereditary ring is not necessarily hereditary. This is in contrast with the fact that graded regular rings and regular rings which are graded (i.e., their (graded) global dimension is finite) coincide.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a strongly <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. By Dade’s theorem (see Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Graded algebras ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), the categories <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent. An application of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Proposition 2.7. ‣ 2.5. Graded hereditary rings ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> shows that if <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is hereditary, then <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded hereditary ring. The
<math id="S2.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(x^{-1})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(x^{-1})=-1</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongly graded ring with the zero-component the ring <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> which is hereditary (PID rings are hereditary). Thus <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded hereditary ring. However, <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is not hereditary: for example, the (non-graded) ideal <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle 2,x-1\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle 2,x-1\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is not a projective module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the graded ring <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a field, is indeed a PID ring and thus a hereditary ring. We will see the graded rings coming out of the graded Bergman constructions are indeed hereditary and thus they are graded hereditary as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6. </span>Smash products of graded algebras</h3>

<section id="S2.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.6.1. </span>Smash products</h4>

<div id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring. The <span id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smash product</span> ring <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the set of all formal sums <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{(\gamma)}\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{(\gamma)}\in R</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are symbols, and all but finitely many coefficients <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero. Addition is defined component-wise and multiplication is defined by linear extension of the rule <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(rp_{\alpha})(sp_{\beta})=rs_{\alpha-\beta}p_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(rp_{\alpha})(sp_{\beta})=rs_{\alpha-\beta}p_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,s\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,s\in R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1p_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1p_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> we may write <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra where <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a (not-necessarily unital) <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra with scalar multiplication <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}=\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\lambda r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}=\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\lambda r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.SSS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}\in R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}\in R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra where <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has the presentation</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R\#\Gamma=\Big\langle rp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid{}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R\#\Gamma=\Big\langle rp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid{}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle rp_{\gamma}+sp_{\gamma}=(r+s)p_{\gamma}\quad(r,s\in R_{\delta};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle rp_{\gamma}+sp_{\gamma}=(r+s)p_{\gamma}\quad(r,s\in R_{\delta};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle~rp_{\gamma}sp_{\delta}=rs_{\gamma-\delta}p_{\delta}\quad(r,s\in R^{h};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.497em">;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~rp_{\gamma}sp_{\delta}=rs_{\gamma-\delta}p_{\delta}\quad(r,s\in R^{h};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
