<section id="S2.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.6.3. </span>An isomorphism of categories</h4>

<div id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the category of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><mi>proj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}</annotation></semantics></math> the full subcategory of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are the projective objects of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> that are finitely generated as a <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the category of unital <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-modules and <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><mi>proj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}}</annotation></semantics></math> the full subcategory of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are the projective objects of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math> that are finitely generated as a <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-module. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> it was shown that there is an isomorphism <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\to R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\to R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> it was shown that <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to an isomorphism <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\to R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><mi>proj</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\to R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><mi>proj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The isomorphism <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> maps an object <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the object <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined as follows. As abelian group <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\#\Gamma=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\#\Gamma=M</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(rp_{\alpha})m=rm_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(rp_{\alpha})m=rm_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M=M\#\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M=M\#\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. On morphisms, <math id="S2.SS6.SSS3.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is just the identity map.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Leavitt path algebras</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a field. Consider the symbols
<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(x_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(x_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}=(x^{*}_{ji})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}=(x^{*}_{ji})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n+k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\left(\begin{matrix}x_{11\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x_{12\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x_{1,n+k}\\
x_{21\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x_{22\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x_{2,n+k}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{n,1\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x_{n,2\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x_{n,n+k}\end{matrix}\right),\qquad X^{*}=\left(\begin{matrix}x^{*}_{11\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x^{*}_{12\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x^{*}_{1,n\phantom{n+{},}}\\
x^{*}_{21\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x^{*}_{22\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x^{*}_{2,n\phantom{n+{},}}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x^{*}_{n+k,1}&amp;x^{*}_{n+k,2}&amp;\dots&amp;x^{*}_{n+k,n}\end{matrix}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>21</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>22</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>11</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>21</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>22</mn><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1.33em;" width="1.33em"></mspace></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\left(\begin{matrix}x_{11\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x_{12\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x_{1,n+k}\\
x_{21\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x_{22\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x_{2,n+k}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x_{n,1\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x_{n,2\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x_{n,n+k}\end{matrix}\right),\qquad X^{*}=\left(\begin{matrix}x^{*}_{11\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x^{*}_{12\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x^{*}_{1,n\phantom{n+{},}}\\
x^{*}_{21\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;x^{*}_{22\phantom{n+{},}}&amp;\dots&amp;x^{*}_{2,n\phantom{n+{},}}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
x^{*}_{n+k,1}&amp;x^{*}_{n+k,2}&amp;\dots&amp;x^{*}_{n+k,n}\end{matrix}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Leavitt algebra</em> <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)=\frac{\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle}{\langle XX^{*}=I_{n},\,X^{*}X=I_{n+k}\rangle}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)=\frac{\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle}{\langle XX^{*}=I_{n},\,X^{*}X=I_{n+k}\rangle}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the free <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra generated by the symbols <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{ji}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{ji}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>’s. Furthermore, the relations <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="XX^{*}=I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">XX^{*}=I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}X=I_{n+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}X=I_{n+k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(\mathsf{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(\mathsf{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n+k}\in\mathbb{M}_{n+k}(\mathsf{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n+k}\in\mathbb{M}_{n+k}(\mathsf{k})</annotation></semantics></math> are the identity matrices, stand for the collection of relations after multiplying the matrices and comparing the two sides of the equations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These rings were first studied by Leavitt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, p.130, footnote 6]</cite>. Leavitt showed the type of the ring <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, is a domain, whereas <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, is a simple ring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) we only consider the relation <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}X=I_{n+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}X=I_{n+k}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we call the resulting algebra <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cohn algebra</em></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)=\frac{\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle}{\langle X^{*}X=I_{n+k}\rangle}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)=\frac{\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle}{\langle X^{*}X=I_{n+k}\rangle},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which was considered by Cohn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, who showed that
<math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, is a domain, whereas <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> has only one non-trivial proper ideal generated by relation <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="XX^{*}=I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">XX^{*}=I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This result also implies the simplicity of <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The simple rings <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> received significant attentions as they are the discrete versions of Cuntz algebras. Modeled on this, Leavitt path algebras are introduced <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, which attach to a directed graph a certain algebra. In the case that the graph has one vertex and <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> loops, it recovers Leavitt algebra <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In order to recover the Leavitt algebras <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, a plethora of generalisations of Leavitt path algebras were introduced, such as Leavitt path algebra of separated graphs, weighted graphs and ultra graphs.
Here, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, we introduce the Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs. We show that Bergman algebras (see Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) with coefficients in a commutative semisimple ring are precisely Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 4.10. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For sets <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, a function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x:I\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x:I\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\mapsto x_{i}=x(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\mapsto x_{i}=x(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">family of elements in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A family <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of elements in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is usually denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call the family <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i})_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i})_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-empty</span> if <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(directed) hypergraph</span> is a quadruple <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(H^{0},H^{1},s,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(H^{0},H^{1},s,r)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are sets and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> are maps associating to each <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a non-empty family <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(h)=(s(h)_{i})_{i\in I_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(h)=(s(h)_{i})_{i\in I_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(h)=(r(h)_{j})_{j\in J_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(h)=(r(h)_{j})_{j\in J_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of elements in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The elements of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are called <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vertices</span> and the elements of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hyperedges</span>. In this paper all hypergraphs are assumed to be <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it regular}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑟𝑒𝑔𝑢𝑙𝑎𝑟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it regular}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. for any hyperedge <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> the sets <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_deff">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a hypergraph. The not necessarily unital <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> presented by the generating set</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{v,h_{ij},h_{ij}^{*}\mid v\in H^{0},h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v,h_{ij},h_{ij}^{*}\mid v\in H^{0},h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the relations</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv=\delta_{uv}u\quad(u,v\in H^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv=\delta_{uv}u\quad(u,v\in H^{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s(h)_{i}h_{ij}=h_{ij}=h_{ij}r(h)_{j},~r(h)_{j}h_{ij}^{*}=h_{ij}^{*}=h_{ij}^{*}s(h)_{i}\quad(h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(h)_{i}h_{ij}=h_{ij}=h_{ij}r(h)_{j},~r(h)_{j}h_{ij}^{*}=h_{ij}^{*}=h_{ij}^{*}s(h)_{i}\quad(h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{j\in J_{h}}h_{ij}h_{i^{\prime}j}^{*}=\delta_{ii^{\prime}}s(h)_{i}\quad(h\in H^{1},~i,i^{\prime}\in I_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{j\in J_{h}}h_{ij}h_{i^{\prime}j}^{*}=\delta_{ii^{\prime}}s(h)_{i}\quad(h\in H^{1},~i,i^{\prime}\in I_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\limits_{i\in I_{h}}h_{ij}^{*}h_{ij^{\prime}}=\delta_{jj^{\prime}}r(h)_{j}\quad(h\in H^{1},~j,j^{\prime}\in J_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\limits_{i\in I_{h}}h_{ij}^{*}h_{ij^{\prime}}=\delta_{jj^{\prime}}r(h)_{j}\quad(h\in H^{1},~j,j^{\prime}\in J_{h})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is called the <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Leavitt path algebra of the hypergraph <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math></span>. We will sometimes call a Leavitt path algebra of a hypergraph a <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hyper Leavitt path algebra</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is not difficult to check that if <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a hypergraph, then <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> has a set of local units, namely the set of all finite sums of distinct elements
of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{K}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{K}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is a unital ring if and only if <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Proposition 8]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a hypergraph. We can describe the relations (ii)-(iv) in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs ‣ 3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> using matrices as follows. For any hyperedge <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}\times I_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}\times I_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix whose entry at position <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,i^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,i^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{ii^{\prime}}s(h)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{ii^{\prime}}s(h)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h}\times J_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h}\times J_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix whose entry at position <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{jj^{\prime}}r(h)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{jj^{\prime}}r(h)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we denote for any hyperedge <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}\times J_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}\times J_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix whose entry at position <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> also by <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h}\times I_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h}\times I_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix whose entry at position <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,i)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra presented by the generating set <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{v,h_{ij},h_{ij}^{*}\mid v\in H^{0},h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v,h_{ij},h_{ij}^{*}\mid v\in H^{0},h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and relations</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(I)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the elements of <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise orthogonal idempotents,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(II)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
