<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this note we use categories of left modules over rings. One can also use categories of right modules. In that case, one would have <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{3}_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n})\cong C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>𝗄</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{3}_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n})\cong C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n)</annotation></semantics></math> returning to right <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This example demonstrates how rich the structure of seemingly easy to construct Bergman algebras of (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In Definition 4.1. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) could be. Consider the Bergman algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity matrix of the <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. One can immediately see that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)\cong\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})*_{\mathsf{k}}\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)\cong\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})*_{\mathsf{k}}\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the discussion in Section 4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> shows that the Leavitt algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with a corner of this Bergman algebra, specifically,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,2)\cong e_{11}B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)e_{11}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,2)\cong e_{11}B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)e_{11}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra, where <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative ring. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> be idempotent matrices. If idempotent matrices <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}\in\mathbb{M}_{k}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}\in\mathbb{M}_{k}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}\in\mathbb{M}_{l}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}\in\mathbb{M}_{l}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent to <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, then <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}^{i}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}^{i}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra isomorphic to <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We show that if <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\sim e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\sim e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}^{i}(e,f)\cong B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}^{i}(e,f)\cong B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)</annotation></semantics></math>. The general result follows by symmetry.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\sim e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\sim e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are matrices <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{M}_{m\times k}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{M}_{m\times k}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{M}_{k\times m}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{M}_{k\times m}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=xy</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}=yx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}=yx</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the map <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:B_{R}^{i}(e,f)\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:B_{R}^{i}(e,f)\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)</annotation></semantics></math> by sending <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\mapsto xh^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\mapsto xh^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mapsto{h^{*}}^{\prime}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mapsto{h^{*}}^{\prime}y</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is present in the definition of <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}^{i}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}^{i}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math>). The converse map <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by sending <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}\mapsto yh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}\mapsto yh</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{h^{*}}^{\prime}\mapsto h^{*}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>h</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{h^{*}}^{\prime}\mapsto h^{*}x</annotation></semantics></math>. We leave it to the reader to check these assignments respect the defining relations and give a <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra isomorphism between the two algebras.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We note that if the idempotents <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent, i.e., <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=hh^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=hh^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=h^{*}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=h^{*}h</annotation></semantics></math>, then by considering <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}=ehf" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}=ehf</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}^{*}=fhe" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}^{*}=fhe</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=h_{1}h_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=h_{1}h_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=h_{1}^{*}h_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=h_{1}^{*}h_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with, <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="eh_{1}f=h_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">eh_{1}f=h_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="fh_{1}^{*}e=h_{1}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">fh_{1}^{*}e=h_{1}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. However the algebras</p>
