<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h,fh^{*}e=h^{*},hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=f\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h,fh^{*}e=h^{*},hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=f\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">are not necessarily isomorphic, as the following example shows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a field (or more generally an integral domain). Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=f\in\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=f\in\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be the idempotent matrix which has a one at position <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and zeros elsewhere (note that the projective <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is just <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the free <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-module of rank 1).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be the free <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{*}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{*}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the relations <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=e,ehe=h,eh^{*}e=h^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=e,ehe=h,eh^{*}e=h^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from the relations <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ehe=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ehe=h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="eh^{*}e=h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">eh^{*}e=h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that all entries of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that are not at position <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> are zero. So <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is just the Laurent polynomial ring <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the free <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{*}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{*}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the relations <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=e\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=e\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=hh^{*}=hh^{*}hh^{*}=heh^{*}=heeh^{*}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=hh^{*}=hh^{*}hh^{*}=heh^{*}=heeh^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">implies <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=eheeh^{*}e," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=eheeh^{*}e,</annotation></semantics></math> it follows that <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{11}h^{*}_{11}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{11}h^{*}_{11}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. But we also have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{11}h^{*}_{11}+h_{12}h^{*}_{21}=(hh^{*})_{11}=e_{11}=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{11}h^{*}_{11}+h_{12}h^{*}_{21}=(hh^{*})_{11}=e_{11}=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and hence <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{12}h^{*}_{21}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{12}h^{*}_{21}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If both <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-zero in <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then the ring <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has zero divisors, while <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> has not. Suppose now that one of the entries <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero in <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{21}h_{12}+h^{*}_{22}h_{22}=(h^{*}h)_{22}=e_{22}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{21}h_{12}+h^{*}_{22}h_{22}=(h^{*}h)_{22}=e_{22}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.4" class="ltx_p">and hence <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{22}h_{22}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{22}h_{22}=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{12}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{12}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{21}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{21}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-zero in <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has zero divisors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the ring obtained from <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> by adjoining two elements <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yz=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yz=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="zy=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">zy=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> are non-zero in <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since</p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(x,y)\operatorname{diag}(x^{-1},z)=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)=\operatorname{diag}(x^{-1},z)\operatorname{diag}(x,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(x,y)\operatorname{diag}(x^{-1},z)=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)=\operatorname{diag}(x^{-1},z)\operatorname{diag}(x,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">there is a ring homomorphism <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\rightarrow R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\rightarrow R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping any <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in R</annotation></semantics></math> to itself, and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{11}\mapsto x,h^{*}_{11}\mapsto x^{-1},h_{22}\mapsto y,h^{*}_{22}\mapsto z," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{11}\mapsto x,h^{*}_{11}\mapsto x^{-1},h_{22}\mapsto y,h^{*}_{22}\mapsto z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.3" class="ltx_p">and the other entries of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero. Since the image of <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> under this homomorphism is non-zero, <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> must be non-zero as well. Similarly it follows that <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>22</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-zero.
Thus <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has zero divisors while <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> has not.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The rings introduced in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, come directly from Bergman’s machinery of universal constructions, by working with idempotent matrices instead of projective modules. Let <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be non-zero finitely generated projective <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, Bergman constructed a ring</p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{1}=R\big\langle h:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}\big\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}=R\big\langle h:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">such that there is a “universal” <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphism
<math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:S_{1}\otimes_{R}P\rightarrow S_{1}\otimes_{R}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:S_{1}\otimes_{R}P\rightarrow S_{1}\otimes_{R}Q</annotation></semantics></math>. He further constructed universal algebras</p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{2}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q},\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P}\big\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q},\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P}\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{3}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q},\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P},h^{*}h=1\big\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{3}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q},\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P},h^{*}h=1\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.4" class="ltx_p">and finally</p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S=S_{4}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S_{4}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.5" class="ltx_p">Thus, over <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Representing <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> as idempotent matrices <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, in §<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> we will establish that the rings <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, above coincide with the Bergman algebras <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{i}_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{i}_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, which explains our terminology. In fact in §<a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> we will carry out the graded version of these constructions. By setting the grade group trivial we obtain the above statements. We note that the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> works with right modules whereas we work here with left modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We can extend the definition of Bergman algebras to families of pairs of idempotents. Let
<math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math>, be a collection of pairs of idempotents. Then we define</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{R}(e,f):=R\big\langle h_{i},h_{i}^{*}\mid i\in I\big\rangle\big/\big\langle e_{i}h_{i}f_{i}=h_{i},f_{i}h_{i}^{*}e_{i}=h_{i}^{*},h_{i}h_{i}^{*}=e_{i},h_{i}^{*}h_{i}=f_{i}\mid i\in I\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f):=R\big\langle h_{i},h_{i}^{*}\mid i\in I\big\rangle\big/\big\langle e_{i}h_{i}f_{i}=h_{i},f_{i}h_{i}^{*}e_{i}=h_{i}^{*},h_{i}h_{i}^{*}=e_{i},h_{i}^{*}h_{i}=f_{i}\mid i\in I\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly we define <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{i}_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{i}_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> as well as <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> be the collection <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(1,1),(1,1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(1,1),(1,1)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathsf{k}}(e,f)=\mathsf{k}\langle x,x^{-1},y,y^{-1}\rangle," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathsf{k}}(e,f)=\mathsf{k}\langle x,x^{-1},y,y^{-1}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math> the (non-commutative) Laurent polynomial ring with two variables.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Next we define the graded version of the Bergman algebras of Definition <a href="#S4.E1" title="In Definition 4.1. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_deff">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra, where <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative ring. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)(\overline{\beta})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)(\overline{\beta})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\gamma})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\gamma})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be idempotent matrices, where <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\beta}=(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\beta}=(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\gamma}=(\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\gamma}=(\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, let
<math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=(h_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=(h_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}=(h^{*}_{ji})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}=(h^{*}_{ji})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, be a collection of symbols. To the symbols
<math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> assign the degree <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{j}-\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{j}-\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math> the degree <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}-\gamma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}-\gamma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Bergman algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}^{i}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}^{i}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> form <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras. For
<math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math>, a collection of pairs of homogeneous idempotents, the graded Bergman algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{i}_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{i}_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined similarly as in (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>)</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It has already been established in the literature that Leavitt path algebras can be realised as Bergman algebras (using the module theoretical language). This identification was then used to describe the non-stable <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of Leavitt path algebras (Theorem 3.2.5 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is field and <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> some finite set. For any <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the element of <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-component is <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and whose other components are <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then any idempotent matrix with entries in <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to a matrix of the form <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dots,t_{n}\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dots,t_{n}\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that there is a <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-1</annotation></semantics></math> correspondence between equivalence classes of idempotent matrices over <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and isomorphism classes of finitely generated projective <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. For any <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}=R\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}=R\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is well-known that any finitely generated projective <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a direct sum <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P_{t_{1}}\oplus\dots\oplus P_{t_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P_{t_{1}}\oplus\dots\oplus P_{t_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\dots,t_{n}\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\dots,t_{n}\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that the corresponding idempotent matrix equals (up to equivalence) <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{t_{1}})\oplus\dots\oplus(\epsilon_{t_{n}})=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{t_{1}})\oplus\dots\oplus(\epsilon_{t_{n}})=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows that if the <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-semisimple, then the Bergman algebras <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) coincide with the class of hyper Leavitt path algebras of Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs ‣ 3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> an abelian group and <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a commutative semisimple <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra considered as <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded algebra concentrated in degree zero. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of pairs of homogeneous idempotents. Then the Bergman algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in </span>(<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>)<span id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded isomorphic to a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> whose grading is induced by a weight map. Conversely, any unital hyper Leavitt path algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> whose grading is induced by a weight map is graded isomorphic to a Bergman algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative semisimple <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra concentrated in degree zero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">First we show that if <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative semisimple <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra concentrated in degree zero, then <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> is graded isomorphic to a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> whose grading is induced by a weight map. Write <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is some finite set. For any <math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the element of <math id="S4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="S4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-component is <math id="S4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and whose other components are <math id="S4.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, write <math id="S4.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f)=\{(e_{u},f_{u})\}_{u\in U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f)=\{(e_{u},f_{u})\}_{u\in U}</annotation></semantics></math>, where for any <math id="S4.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{m_{u}}(R)(\overline{\alpha}_{u})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{m_{u}}(R)(\overline{\alpha}_{u})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{u}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n_{u}}(R)(\overline{\beta}_{u})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{u}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n_{u}}(R)(\overline{\beta}_{u})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}_{u}\in\Gamma^{m_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}_{u}\in\Gamma^{m_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\beta}_{u}\in\Gamma^{n_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\beta}_{u}\in\Gamma^{n_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, are homogeneous idempotent matrices. Since <math id="S4.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, by Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 4.5. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 4.9. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a>, we may assume that for any <math id="S4.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{u,1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{u,m_{u}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{u,1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{u,m_{u}}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{u}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t^{\prime}_{u,1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t^{\prime}_{u,n_{u}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{u}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t^{\prime}_{u,1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t^{\prime}_{u,n_{u}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S4.p15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{u,1},\dots,t_{u,m_{u}},t^{\prime}_{u,1},\dots,t^{\prime}_{u,n_{u}}\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{u,1},\dots,t_{u,m_{u}},t^{\prime}_{u,1},\dots,t^{\prime}_{u,n_{u}}\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a hypergraph <math id="S4.p15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(H^{0},H^{1},s,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(H^{0},H^{1},s,r)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}=\{v_{t}\mid t\in T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}=\{v_{t}\mid t\in T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}=\{h_{u}\mid u\in U\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}=\{h_{u}\mid u\in U\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h_{u}}=\{1,\dots,m_{u}\}~(h_{u}\in H^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h_{u}}=\{1,\dots,m_{u}\}~(h_{u}\in H^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h_{u}}=\{1,\dots,n_{u}\}~(h_{u}\in H^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h_{u}}=\{1,\dots,n_{u}\}~(h_{u}\in H^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(h_{u})_{i}=v_{t_{u,i}}~(h_{u}\in H^{1},i\in I_{h_{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(h_{u})_{i}=v_{t_{u,i}}~(h_{u}\in H^{1},i\in I_{h_{u}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(u)_{j}=v_{t^{\prime}_{u,j}}~(h_{u}\in H^{1},j\in J_{h_{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(u)_{j}=v_{t^{\prime}_{u,j}}~(h_{u}\in H^{1},j\in J_{h_{u}})</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Remark 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs ‣ 3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> that <math id="S4.p15.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)\cong L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)\cong L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p15.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras. Since <math id="S4.p15.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}=T</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, <math id="S4.p15.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is unital (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Proposition 8]</cite>). Now define a <math id="S4.p15.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-weight map <math id="S4.p15.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p15.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p15.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w((h_{u})_{ij})=(\beta_{u})_{j}-(\alpha_{u})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w((h_{u})_{ij})=(\beta_{u})_{j}-(\alpha_{u})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p15.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{u}\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{u}\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I_{h_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I_{h_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in J_{h_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in J_{h_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One checks easily that <math id="S4.p15.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed a <math id="S4.p15.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-weight map for <math id="S4.p15.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the isomorphism <math id="S4.p15.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}(e,f)\cong L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}(e,f)\cong L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is graded with respect to the <math id="S4.p15.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-grading on <math id="S4.p15.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S4.p15.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now let <math id="S4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> be a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra whose grading is induced by a <math id="S4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-weight map <math id="S4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is unital, <math id="S4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. Let <math id="S4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:=\Pi_{v\in H^{0}}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:=\Pi_{v\in H^{0}}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S4.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in H^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in H^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S4.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> be the element of <math id="S4.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> whose <math id="S4.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>-component is <math id="S4.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and whose other components are <math id="S4.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We may assume that for any <math id="S4.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{h}=\{1,\dots,m_{h}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{h}=\{1,\dots,m_{h}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h}=\{1,\dots,n_{h}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h}=\{1,\dots,n_{h}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and moreover that <math id="S4.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{h}=1=j_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{h}=1=j_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S4.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-weight map for <math id="S4.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(h_{ij})=w(h_{i1})-w(h_{11})+w(h_{1j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(h_{ij})=w(h_{i1})-w(h_{11})+w(h_{1j})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S4.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> set <math id="S4.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{h,i}:=-w(h_{i1})+w(h_{11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{h,i}:=-w(h_{i1})+w(h_{11})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{h,j}:=w(h_{1j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{h,j}:=w(h_{1j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, set <math id="S4.p16.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}_{h}:=(\alpha_{h,1},\dots,\alpha_{h,m_{h}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}_{h}:=(\alpha_{h,1},\dots,\alpha_{h,m_{h}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\beta}_{h}:=(\beta_{h,1},\dots,\beta_{h,n_{h}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\beta}_{h}:=(\beta_{h,1},\dots,\beta_{h,n_{h}})</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S4.p16.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S4.p16.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{h}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{m_{h}}(R)(\overline{\alpha}_{h})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{h}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{m_{h}}(R)(\overline{\alpha}_{h})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the idempotent matrix whose entry at position <math id="S4.p16.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,i^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,i^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p16.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{ii^{\prime}}\epsilon_{s(h)_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{ii^{\prime}}\epsilon_{s(h)_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p16.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{h}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n_{h}}(R)(\overline{\beta}_{h})_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{h}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n_{h}}(R)(\overline{\beta}_{h})_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the idempotent matrix whose entry at position <math id="S4.p16.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p16.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{jj^{\prime}}\epsilon_{r(h)_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{jj^{\prime}}\epsilon_{r(h)_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Remark 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs ‣ 3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> that <math id="S4.p16.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)\cong B_{R}(e,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)\cong B_{R}(e,f)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p16.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras, where <math id="S4.p16.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f)=\{(e_{h},f_{h})\}_{h\in H^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f)=\{(e_{h},f_{h})\}_{h\in H^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One checks easily that this isomorphism is graded as well.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
