<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)=(g\circ\varepsilon)(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)=(g\circ\varepsilon)(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(p)=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(p)=p</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\circ h=h=g\circ\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\circ h=h=g\circ\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-e)\circ h=(1-e)\circ e\circ h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-e)\circ h=(1-e)\circ e\circ h=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ u=g\circ\varepsilon\circ u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ u=g\circ\varepsilon\circ u=0</annotation></semantics></math>. For the uniqueness of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose that there exists <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}:X\xrightarrow{}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}:X\xrightarrow{}F</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\circ h^{\prime}=g\circ\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\circ h^{\prime}=g\circ\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}\circ u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}\circ u=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-e)\circ h^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-e)\circ h^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}(x)=h^{\prime}(x)-((1-e)\circ h^{\prime})(x)=e\circ h^{\prime})(x)=(g\circ\varepsilon)(x)=h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}(x)=h^{\prime}(x)-((1-e)\circ h^{\prime})(x)=e\circ h^{\prime})(x)=(g\circ\varepsilon)(x)=h(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}=h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}=h</annotation></semantics></math>. One observes that if <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\xrightarrow{}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\xrightarrow{}P</annotation></semantics></math> is the zero map, one takes <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> to be the zero map which is the desired map. Obviously if <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:X\xrightarrow{}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:X\xrightarrow{}F</annotation></semantics></math> is the zero map, then <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, when <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ u=0</annotation></semantics></math> by the universal property of cokernel there exists <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:M\xrightarrow{}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:M\xrightarrow{}F</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\tau\circ\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\tau\circ\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=e\circ\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=e\circ\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\circ h=g\circ\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\circ h=g\circ\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. For the uniqueness of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose there exists another <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}:M\xrightarrow{}{}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}:M\xrightarrow{}{}P</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\circ h=g^{\prime}\circ\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\circ h=g^{\prime}\circ\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ\varepsilon=e\circ h=g^{\prime}\circ\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ\varepsilon=e\circ h=g^{\prime}\circ\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, we have <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We are in position to extend two crucial theorems of Bergman on universal ring constructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorems 3.1 and 3.2]</cite> to the setting of graded rings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Then there exists a <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, with a universal graded module homomorphism <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S\otimes_{R}M\rightarrow{}S\otimes_{R}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S\otimes_{R}M\rightarrow{}S\otimes_{R}P</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, given any <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and any graded <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphism <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:T\otimes_{R}M\rightarrow{}T\otimes_{R}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:T\otimes_{R}M\rightarrow{}T\otimes_{R}P</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique graded homomorphism <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\rightarrow{}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\rightarrow{}T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=T\otimes_{S}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=T\otimes_{S}f</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first write <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as a cokernel of the graded <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphism <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> of graded free <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules; that is</span></p>
