<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the choices of resolutions for <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. One observes that <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has the universal property for fixed resolutions of <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that applying the forgetful functor <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with a universal graded module homomorphism in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> we have the ring in Bergman’s Theorem 3.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Actually when we apply the forgetful functor <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> to the graded projective resolution <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i})\xrightarrow{u}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})\xrightarrow{\varepsilon}M\xrightarrow{}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑢</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜀</mo></mover><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i})\xrightarrow{u}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})\xrightarrow{\varepsilon}M\xrightarrow{}0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the non-graded projective resolution <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i\in I}R\xrightarrow{u}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\xrightarrow{\varepsilon}M\xrightarrow{}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑢</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜀</mo></mover><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i\in I}R\xrightarrow{u}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\xrightarrow{\varepsilon}M\xrightarrow{}0</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly if we apply the forgetful functor <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> to the idempotent endomorphism <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have the idempotent endomorphism <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e:\bigoplus_{K}R\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{K}R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e:\bigoplus_{K}R\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{K}R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given us any <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mtext>ring</mtext></mrow><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and any module homomorphism <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}:T^{\prime}\otimes_{R}M\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime}\otimes_{R}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}:T^{\prime}\otimes_{R}M\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime}\otimes_{R}P</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
