<p id="S5.I1.i2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined by <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; see Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. This <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> gives us a ring homomorphism <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> . Hence <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the universal property as the ring in Bergman’s Theorem 3.1. Therefore <math id="S5.I1.i2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the ring in Bergman’s Theorem 3.1.</p>
