<div id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We write <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as a direct summand of a graded free module <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for any graded <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{i}:T\otimes_{R}P\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}T(\gamma_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{i}:T\otimes_{R}P\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}T(\gamma_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the inclusion <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:P\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:P\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is again an inclusion. Hence for each homogeneous elements <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, we write</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\sum_{m\in K}f_{mx}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\sum_{m\in K}f_{mx}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{mx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{mx}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homogeneous element in <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\gamma_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\gamma_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe that for any <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\otimes_{R}f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\otimes_{R}f=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if for any homogeneous elements <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\bigcup_{\alpha\in\Gamma}M_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\bigcup_{\alpha\in\Gamma}M_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> all the <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{mx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{mx}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero in <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. So the desired universal ring <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient of <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by the two-sided ideal generated by these homogeneous elements <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{mx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{mx}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The ring constructed in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>), and that of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> by <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the graded <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism from <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> is a canonical projection.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span></h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Theorems <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> we can construct graded rings with rich structures.
Let <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:P\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:P\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math> be a module homomorphism between graded finitely generated projective
<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> we construct a graded ring with a homomorphism <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and then by two applications of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> we set <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-g\overline{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-g\overline{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\overline{f}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\overline{f}g</annotation></semantics></math> to zero. We denote the graded ring obtained by <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle{\overline{f}}^{-1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle{\overline{f}}^{-1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> where the homomorphism <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is now invertible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In a similar fashion, for graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we can adjoin a universal graded isomorphism between <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> by first freely adjoining a graded map <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{P}\xrightarrow{}\overline{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{P}\xrightarrow{}\overline{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, then adjoining an inverse. We denote the resulting <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{Q}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>gr</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{Q}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a single graded finitely generated projective module <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, one may obtain a <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> by adjoining to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a universal idempotent graded endomorphism of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. This ring will be denoted by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle i:\overline{P}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P};i^{2}=i\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle i:\overline{P}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P};i^{2}=i\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
