</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Universal morphisms for strongly graded rings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. As mentioned in the introduction, in certain sections, such as §<a href="#S6" title="6. Smash products of graded Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and §<a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra I ‣ 7. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> we need to assume that the graded ring <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is concentrated in degree zero. Here we also need to make this assumption, namely, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra, where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is concentrated in degree zero. Note that, by our assumption <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}\subseteq R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}\subseteq R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. It is natural to compare the Bergman algebras <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P_{0}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P_{0}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, in case <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-module. We will show that if <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly graded, then there is a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded isomorphism of algebras,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f:\overline{M}\longrightarrow\overline{P}\rangle\cong R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\longrightarrow\overline{P_{0}}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f:\overline{M}\longrightarrow\overline{P}\rangle\cong R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\longrightarrow\overline{P_{0}}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This ties the two representable functors (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) together.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider the grading for coproducts of two <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-grading. Then the coproduct <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\ast_{R_{0}}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\ast_{R_{0}}S</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-grading. The coproduct is, as a graded vector space, the space of all words in <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and the homogeneous elements of pure degree are words where the letters are homogeneous, and the degree is given by the sum of the degrees. One observes that when <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> with trivial grading, the zeroth component of the coproduct <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\ast_{R_{0}}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\ast_{R_{0}}S</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}\ast_{R_{0}}S\cong S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}\ast_{R_{0}}S\cong S</annotation></semantics></math>. And the zeroth component of <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\ast_{R_{0}}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\ast_{R_{0}}S</annotation></semantics></math> can contain elements such as <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rsr^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rsr^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,r^{\prime}\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,r^{\prime}\in R</annotation></semantics></math> are homogeneous and the sum of the degrees are zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring concentrated in degree zero, and <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a strongly <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module and <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Then we have the following isomorphism of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\longrightarrow\overline{P_{0}}\rangle\cong R\langle f:\overline{M}\longrightarrow\overline{P}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\longrightarrow\overline{P_{0}}\rangle\cong R\langle f:\overline{M}\longrightarrow\overline{P}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose a projective resolution</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i\in I}R_{0}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle u_{0}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R_{0}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\varepsilon_{0}}}{{\longrightarrow}}M_{0}\longrightarrow 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mover><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mover><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i\in I}R_{0}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle u_{0}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R_{0}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\varepsilon_{0}}}{{\longrightarrow}}M_{0}\longrightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
