<p id="S5.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">such that <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\theta\circ\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\theta\circ\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\theta\circ\pi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\theta\circ\pi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. First we define <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m32" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\theta:R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle\xrightarrow{}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle\xrightarrow{}S</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(r)=\beta(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(r)=\beta(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(h_{ij})=\alpha(h_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(h_{ij})=\alpha(h_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=(h_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=(h_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined as <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> sends the generators of the two-sided ideal <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle u_{0}h_{0}=0=h_{0}(1-e_{0})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle u_{0}h_{0}=0=h_{0}(1-e_{0})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> to zero. And we can check directly that <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\theta\circ\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\theta\circ\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\theta\circ\pi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\theta\circ\pi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The uniqueness of <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> follows directly from the commutativity of the two triangles in the above diagram. One observes that <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the grading in the graded algebras setting. Therefore, we obtain that <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m44" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P_{0}}\rangle\cong R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle f_{0}:\overline{M_{0}}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P_{0}}\rangle\cong R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras.
∎</span></p>
