<p id="S5.SS2.p9.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p9.5.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:R_{0}\xrightarrow{}R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:R_{0}\xrightarrow{}R</annotation></semantics></math> is the inclusion and <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:R_{0}\xrightarrow{}R_{0}\langle\overline{f_{0}}=0\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:R_{0}\xrightarrow{}R_{0}\langle\overline{f_{0}}=0\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the natural projection. One can check directly that <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the universal property of <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle\overline{f_{0}}=0\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\ast_{R_{0}}R_{0}\langle\overline{f_{0}}=0\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras. The proof is completed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a strongly <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Suppose that <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Then we have the following two universal algebras:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{0}\langle i_{0},i^{-1}_{0}:\overline{M}_{0}\cong\overline{P}_{0}\rangle\text{ and }R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{M}\cong\overline{P}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}\langle i_{0},i^{-1}_{0}:\overline{M}_{0}\cong\overline{P}_{0}\rangle\text{ and }R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{M}\cong\overline{P}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">It would be interesting to establish a relation between these two algebras and <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{M}\cong\overline{P}\rangle_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{M}\cong\overline{P}\rangle_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> similar to Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 5.5. ‣ 5.2. Universal morphisms for strongly graded rings ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian group, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative ring and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be non-zero graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Applying Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> we obtain a <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle i:\overline{P}\xrightarrow{}\overline{Q}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle i:\overline{P}\xrightarrow{}\overline{Q}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from §<a href="#S5.SS1.SSS1" title="5.1.1. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.1</span></a> that we can also construct the graded rings <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{Q}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>gr</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle i,i^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{Q}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\big\langle i:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~i^{2}=i\big\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.497em">;</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\big\langle i:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~i^{2}=i\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further if <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:P\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:P\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorhism, then <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle\overline{g}^{-1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle\overline{g}^{-1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal ring which <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> becomes invertible. In this section, by representing the graded finitely generated projective modules by idempotent matrices,
we describe these <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded rings via generators and relations,
in Lemmas <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 5.8. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>, <a href="#S5.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 5.9. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 5.10. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>. Once the grade group <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is set to be trivial, we obtain the Bergman algebras of Definition <a href="#S4.E1" title="In Definition 4.1. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is graded finitely generated <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module, using (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.3. Matrix form of graded homomorphisms ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>), we can write <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as the image of a graded idempotent endomorphism <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of a graded free <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite rank <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}R(\beta_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}R(\beta_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{j}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{j}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we write <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> as the image of a graded idempotent endomorphism <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> of a graded free <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite rank <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{k=1}^{m}R(\gamma_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{k=1}^{m}R(\gamma_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{k}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{k}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> there is a unique graded homomorphism <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{g}:\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}R(\beta_{j})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{k=1}^{m}R(\gamma_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{g}:\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}R(\beta_{j})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{k=1}^{m}R(\gamma_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the given graded module homomorphism <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:P\xrightarrow{}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:P\xrightarrow{}Q</annotation></semantics></math> such that we have the following diagram</p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

