<div id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 5.7. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>(1) we have <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{1}=R\langle i:\overline{Q}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle\cong R\langle h^{*}\rangle/fh^{*}e=h^{*}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}=R\langle i:\overline{Q}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle\cong R\langle h^{*}\rangle/fh^{*}e=h^{*}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly as in Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 5.8. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>, one has that <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}\circ h_{g}-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}\circ h_{g}-e</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique graded homomorphism corresponding to <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\circ(S_{1}\otimes_{R}g)-1_{S_{1}\otimes_{R}P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\circ(S_{1}\otimes_{R}g)-1_{S_{1}\otimes_{R}P}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{g}\circ h^{*}-f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{g}\circ h^{*}-f</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique graded homomorphism corresponding to <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{1}\otimes_{R}g)\circ i-1_{S_{1}\otimes_{R}Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{1}\otimes_{R}g)\circ i-1_{S_{1}\otimes_{R}Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. We omit the details here.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Universal morphisms and representable functors</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall some facts on representable functors and universal morphisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a small category and <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:\mathcal{C}\xrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:\mathcal{C}\xrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> a functor, where <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Sets</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> is the category of sets with set functions as morphisms. We say that <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representable</em> if there exists a natural isomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F</annotation></semantics></math> for some object <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. For an element <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that an element <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in F(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in F(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal</em> if for any object <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in F(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in F(B)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a unique morphism <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:A\xrightarrow{}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:A\xrightarrow{}B</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=F(f)(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=F(f)(u)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, this property is called the universal property of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,u)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an object in <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. The representations <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F</annotation></semantics></math> are in bijection to universal elements of the form <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,u)</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\xrightarrow{}\alpha_{A}(1_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\xrightarrow{}\alpha_{A}(1_{A})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Yoneda Lemma, the natural transformations <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to elements in <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(A)</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\mapsto\alpha_{A}(1_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\mapsto\alpha_{A}(1_{A})</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains to check that <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism if and only if <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}(1_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}(1_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is an universal element. Actually, for any object <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{B}:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)\xrightarrow{}F(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{B}:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)\xrightarrow{}F(B)</annotation></semantics></math>. By the naturality of <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, for any morphism <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:A\xrightarrow{}B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:A\xrightarrow{}B</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{B}(f)=\alpha_{B}(f\circ 1_{A})=F(f)(\alpha_{A}(1_{A}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{B}(f)=\alpha_{B}(f\circ 1_{A})=F(f)(\alpha_{A}(1_{A}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a natural isomorphism if and only if for any object <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)\xrightarrow{}F(B),f\mapsto F(f)(\alpha_{A}(1_{A}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)\xrightarrow{}F(B),f\mapsto F(f)(\alpha_{A}(1_{A}))</annotation></semantics></math> is bijection, which is a restatement of saying that <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}(1_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}(1_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a universal element.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In what follows, we continue with the assumption that <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian group, <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded commutative ring and <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Suppose that <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module and <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> a graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> now says that the functor from <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-}\text{rings}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mtext>rings</mtext></mrow><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-}\text{rings}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Sets</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>, associating to <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}M,T\otimes_{R}P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}M,T\otimes_{R}P)</annotation></semantics></math>, is representable. More precisely, we have the functor</p>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}:R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&amp;\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\
T&amp;\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}M,T\otimes_{R}P)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-gr-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>T</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}:R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&amp;\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\
T&amp;\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}M,T\otimes_{R}P)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}(f)=T^{\prime}\otimes_{T}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}(f)=T^{\prime}\otimes_{T}-</annotation></semantics></math> for any graded <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:T\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:T\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there is a natural isomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}}(S,-)\xrightarrow{}F^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-gr-rings</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>gr</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}}(S,-)\xrightarrow{}F^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> the graded algebra constructed in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We recall from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Chapter 4]</cite> the notion of universal localization of algebras. Let <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be
an algebra and <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of morphisms between finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. A homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:R\xrightarrow{}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:R\xrightarrow{}S</annotation></semantics></math> of algebras is called <em id="S5.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S5.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-inverting</em> if for each morphism <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}\xi</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S5.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Mod}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌𝐨𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Mod}}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible; <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="S5.SS4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal localization
with respect to <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></em> if in addition each <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-inverting homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factors
uniquely through <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt this to the setting of graded algebras. Let <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian group, <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R,</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S5.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> two <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras and <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> a family of morphisms between <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. A homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:R\xrightarrow{}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:R\xrightarrow{}S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded algebras is <em id="S5.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-inverting</em>, if for all <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}\xi</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S5.SS4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible; <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="S5.SS4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded universal localization with repect to <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math></em> if in addition every <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-inverting homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded algebras factors uniquely through <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Proposition 3.1]</cite>, it was shown that, the <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded universal localisation exists. Using our graded constructions, here we show that, for any arbitrary abelian group <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded universal localisation exists.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from §<a href="#S5.SS1.SSS1" title="5.1.1. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1.1</span></a> that given a graded homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:P\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:P\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math> between graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we can construct a graded ring <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle{\overline{f}}^{-1}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle{\overline{f}}^{-1}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> where the homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes invertible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.12</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras and <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> a family of morphisms between <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. If <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta:R\xrightarrow{}{}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta:R\xrightarrow{}{}S</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded universal localization with respect to <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cong R\langle\overline{\xi}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cong R\langle\overline{\xi}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}Q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}Q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, collect the pair of graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{\xi},P_{\xi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{\xi},P_{\xi})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By a repeated application of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5.1. Graded universal morphisms ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> for each pair <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{\xi},P_{\xi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{\xi},P_{\xi})</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring</span></p>
