<p id="S5.SS4.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}R_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}R_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graded algebra homomorphism such that <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\Sigma}\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mtext>ring</mtext></mrow><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\Sigma}\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. From the construction, we have <math id="S5.SS4.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\Sigma}=R\langle\overline{\xi}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\Sigma}=R\langle\overline{\xi}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded localisation ring, for each <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}Q_{\xi}\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}Q_{\xi}\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the isomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\otimes_{R}\xi:S\otimes_{R}P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}S\otimes_{R}Q_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\otimes_{R}\xi:S\otimes_{R}P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}S\otimes_{R}Q_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now by the universal property of <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique graded algebra homomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S\otimes_{R}\xi)^{-1}=S\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S\otimes_{R}\xi)^{-1}=S\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies (<a href="#S5.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.4. Universal morphisms and representable functors ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>), that is,</span></p>
