<p id="S5.SS4.p15.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p15.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\circ\theta_{2}=\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\circ\theta_{2}=\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p15.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p15.5.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore we have <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta=\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta=\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that there exists <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}:R_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}:R_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining with (<a href="#S5.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.4. Universal morphisms and representable functors ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>) we have that <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}=\lambda\circ\theta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}=\lambda\circ\theta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the uniqueness of <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\eta^{\prime}\circ\theta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\eta^{\prime}\circ\theta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By comparing with (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.4. Universal morphisms and representable functors ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>) we have <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we prove that <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}:R\xrightarrow{}R_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}:R\xrightarrow{}R_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded universal localization of <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. The uniqueness of <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> up to isomorphism follows from the universal property of Bergman algebras.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">(2) Take <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to be the trivial group. Then we have the second statement.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">(3) By Bergman’s construction <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle{\overline{\xi}}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle{\overline{\xi}}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is hereditary. As it is a graded ring, it is graded hereditary.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Path algebras as universal algebras</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bergman’s proof of Theorem 5.3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> indicates that any lower triangle matrix ring can be realised as the universal algebra of the form <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f_{i}:\overline{P}_{i}\rightarrow\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f_{i}:\overline{P}_{i}\rightarrow\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen as an appropriate
commutative <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-semisimple ring. Furthermore, inverting these maps, i.e., <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\langle f_{i},f_{i}^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle f_{i},f_{i}^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> would then produce the whole matrix ring. As the lower triangle matrix rings are path algebras of certain kind of line graphs, it is natural to ask whether all path algebras of graphs can be obtained as universal algebras. Here we show this is in fact the case, namely for any finite graph <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, the path algebra <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\cong R\langle f_{i}:\overline{P}_{i}\rightarrow\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\cong R\langle f_{i}:\overline{P}_{i}\rightarrow\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is an commutative <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-semisimple ring and <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i},Q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i},Q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules determined by the shape of the graph. Although it is usually quite difficult to describe the universal ring obtained by inverting morphisms (see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>), here we show that the Leavitt path algebra <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal algebra, which inverts the maps <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:P_{i}\rightarrow Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:P_{i}\rightarrow Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although the results of this section might be known (or predictable) to the experts, we provide the details to demonstrate what it means to build a graded ring from the inset and not to assign the grading from the outset, as it has been done so far in the theory of combinatorial algebras. Namely, previously we would construct (Leavitt) path algebras and then assign a grading to them. By using our graded Bergman machinery, the algebras obtained are already equipped with a grading.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph. We use <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\operatorname{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>reg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\operatorname{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the set of vertices in <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> at which there are at least one edge starting from it.
Recall that the <em id="S5.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">path algebra</em> of the graph <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
