<p id="S5.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}\big\langle E^{0},E^{1}\big\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}\big\langle E^{0},E^{1}\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the free (non-unital) algebra with vertices and edges of the graph as the generators.
Assigning zero to vertices and <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to edges, since the defining relations in (<a href="#S5.E13" title="In 5.5. Path algebras as universal algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>) are homogeneous, <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> becomes a <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring (with positive support).
In Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 5.14. ‣ 5.5. Path algebras as universal algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a> we will realise <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded universal algebra.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the semisimple <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the product of <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E^{0}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E^{0}|</annotation></semantics></math>-copies of the field <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring which is concentrated in the zero component.
Denote <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, the graded finitely generated projective <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module with <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> appears in <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>-th component of <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and zero elsewhere. Throughout, we write <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(i),i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(i),i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the shift of the projective <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}p_{v}\cong R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}p_{v}\cong R</annotation></semantics></math> as graded <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph and <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> a field. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded algebra concentrated in degree zero, with graded finitely generated projective modules <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> as above. Then we have a <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

