<p id="S5.SS5.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e,f)=\{(e_{h^{v}},f_{h^{v}})\,|\,v\in E^{\rm reg}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>reg</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e,f)=\{(e_{h^{v}},f_{h^{v}})\,|\,v\in E^{\rm reg}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{h^{v}}=(\epsilon_{v})\in R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{h^{v}}=(\epsilon_{v})\in R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{h^{v}}=\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{|J_{v}|\times|J_{v}|}(R)(1,1,\cdots,1)_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>v</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{h^{v}}=\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{|J_{v}|\times|J_{v}|}(R)(1,1,\cdots,1)_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the idempotent matrix whose entry at position <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{jj^{\prime}}\epsilon_{r(e^{v,j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{jj^{\prime}}\epsilon_{r(e^{v,j})}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here in the equation (<a href="#S5.E17" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.5. Path algebras as universal algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.17</span></a>) the first isomorphism follows by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm14" title="Lemma 5.14. ‣ 5.5. Path algebras as universal algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>, the second isomorphism follows by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 5.8. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>, the third isomorphism is given in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 4.10. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a> and the last isomorphism is given in Example <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Example 3.4 (Leavitt path algebras). ‣ 3.1. Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs ‣ 3. Leavitt path algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Moreover all the four isomorphisms preserve elements. Hence the composition <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\xrightarrow{}P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\langle\xi^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}L(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mover><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\xrightarrow{}P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\langle\xi^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}L(E)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mapsto p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mapsto p</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded universal localization of <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
