<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Smash products of graded Bergman algebras II</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-zero graded finitely generated projective <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module and set</p>
<table id="S6.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S:=R\big\langle e:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~e^{2}=e\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.497em">;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:=R\big\langle e:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~e^{2}=e\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as the image of an idempotent graded endomorphism <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of a free graded <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module of finite rank <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(d_{ij})=\beta_{j}-\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(d_{ij})=\beta_{j}-\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in J</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 5.9. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a> that <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained from <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by adjoining homogeneous generators <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}~(i,j\in J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}~(i,j\in J)</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(e_{ij})=\beta_{j}-\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(e_{ij})=\beta_{j}-\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and relations <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="de=e=ed" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">de=e=ed</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ee=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ee=e</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cong R\langle X\rangle/I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cong R\langle X\rangle/I</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X=\{e_{ij}\mid i,j\in J\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{e_{ij}\mid i,j\in J\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\langle X\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\langle X\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the relations</p>
