<div id="S6.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">See the proof of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 6.1. ‣ 6.1. Smash products of graded Bergman algebras I ‣ 6. Smash products of graded Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in A</annotation></semantics></math> define the matrices</p>
<table id="S6.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d^{(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d^{(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=(d_{ij}^{(\gamma)})_{i,j\in J}=(d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})_{i,j\in J}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{J\times J}(R\#\Gamma_{A})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=(d_{ij}^{(\gamma)})_{i,j\in J}=(d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})_{i,j\in J}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{J\times J}(R\#\Gamma_{A}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">A straightforward computation shows that the matrices <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math> are idempotent. Hence they define idempotent endomorphisms of <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{A}^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A})d^{(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{A}^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A})d^{(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-zero finitely generated projective <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\#\Gamma_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\#\Gamma_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Define the <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra</p>
<table id="S6.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{A}:=R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle f^{(\gamma)}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\to\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}};~(f^{(\gamma)})^{2}=f^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>#</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.497em">;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{A}:=R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle f^{(\gamma)}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\to\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}};~(f^{(\gamma)})^{2}=f^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p">We will show that <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{A}\cong B_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{A}\cong B_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Lemma 5.8. ‣ 5.3. Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> and (<a href="#S6.E19" title="In Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6.2. Smash products of graded Bergman algebras II ‣ 6. Smash products of graded Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.19</span></a>) we have</p>
<table id="S6.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}B_{A}=\Big\langle&amp;rp_{\beta}~(r\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A});~~~~f_{ij}^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A;~i,j\in J)\mid{}\\
&amp;rp_{\beta}+sp_{\beta}=(r+s)p_{\beta}\quad(r,s\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A}),\\
&amp;rp_{\beta}sp_{\beta^{\prime}}=rs_{\beta-\beta^{\prime}}p_{\beta^{\prime}}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~s\in R_{\alpha^{\prime}};~\alpha+\beta,\beta,\alpha^{\prime}+\beta^{\prime},\beta^{\prime}\in\Gamma_{A}),\\
&amp;\sum_{k\in J}d_{ik}^{(\gamma)}f_{kj}^{(\gamma)}=f_{ij}^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{k\in J}f_{ik}^{(\gamma)}d_{kj}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in A),\\
