<p id="S7.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to show that the isomorphism <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)/\big([P(\gamma)]=[Q(\gamma)]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big)\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)/\big([P(\gamma)]=[Q(\gamma)]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big)\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained above is given by tensoring. Let <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a graded finitely generated projective <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module. By §<a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. Basic definitions and results ‣ 7. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=M(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=M(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> for some idempotent matrix <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\epsilon_{ij})=\gamma_{j}-\gamma_{i}~(1\leq i,j\leq n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\epsilon_{ij})=\gamma_{j}-\gamma_{i}~(1\leq i,j\leq n)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the isomorphisms above in reversed order to <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[M]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)/\big([P(\gamma)]=[Q(\gamma)]~~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[M]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)/\big([P(\gamma)]=[Q(\gamma)]~~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big)</annotation></semantics></math> yields (in view of Lemmas <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7.1. Basic definitions and results ‣ 7. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a grad