<div id="S7.Thmthm7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Returning to the graded setting, we have</p>
<table id="S7.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n}}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n+k}(-1)}\rangle," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>gr</mi></msub><mi>𝗄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>gr</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n}}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n+k}(-1)}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and by Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 7.6. ‣ 7.2. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra I ‣ 7. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a></p>
<table id="S7.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(\mathsf{k})/\langle[\mathsf{k}^{n}]=[\mathsf{k}^{n+k}(-1)]\rangle\cong\big\langle 1,(n/n+k)^{i},i\in\mathbb{Z}\big\rangle\subseteq\mathbb{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(\mathsf{k})/\langle[\mathsf{k}^{n}]=[\mathsf{k}^{n+k}(-1)]\rangle\cong\big\langle 1,(n/n+k)^{i},i\in\mathbb{Z}\big\rangle\subseteq\mathbb{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))]</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in this monoid, which is then a strongly order unit, by Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 2.6. ‣ 2.4. Non-stable Graded 𝐾-theory ‣ 2. Γ-monoids and graded algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, we conclude that <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongly graded ring. (This can also be obtained from looking at the generators and relations of the algebra.) Therefore, an application of Dade’s theorem, imply that
<math id="S7.Thmthm7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn implies that the zero component ring <math id="S7.Thmthm7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has IBN.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>The graded <math id="S7.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra II</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra where <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an abelian group and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> a field concentrated in degree zero. Moreover, let <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-zero graded finitely generated projective <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Set</p>
