</div>
<div id="S8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p9.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a finite number of elements whose sum is the order unit <math id="S8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, then the construction of the algebra <math id="S8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 8.1. ‣ 8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a> above simplifies a bit, as it is demonstrated in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 8.2. ‣ 8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,i)</annotation></semantics></math> be a pointed conical <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid with a distinguished order unit <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by finitely many elements whose sum is <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a hereditary unital hyper Leavitt path algebra <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> with a <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-grading induced by a weight map, such that <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:(M,i)\cong(\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R),[R])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:(M,i)\cong(\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R),[R])</annotation></semantics></math> as pointed <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids. Furthermore, <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> has a weak universal property.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{p_{\phi}\mid\phi\in\Phi\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{p_{\phi}\mid\phi\in\Phi\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set, be a set non-zero generators for <math id="S8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\phi\in\Phi}p_{\phi}=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\phi\in\Phi}p_{\phi}=i</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\{u_{\psi}=v_{\psi}\mid\psi\in\Psi\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\{u_{\psi}=v_{\psi}\mid\psi\in\Psi\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of relations such that <math id="S8.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\langle X\mid Y\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\langle X\mid Y\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S8.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is conical, we may assume that <math id="S8.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\psi},v_{\psi}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\psi},v_{\psi}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S8.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S8.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S8.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S8.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}^{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝗄</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}^{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> which is concentrated in degree <math id="S8.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R^{\prime})=\big\langle P_{\phi}~(\phi\in\Phi)\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R^{\prime})=\big\langle P_{\phi}~(\phi\in\Phi)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">is freely generated by generators <math id="S8.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}~(\phi\in\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}~(\phi\in\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> whose sum is <math id="S8.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all finite subsets of <math id="S8.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly <math id="S8.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed set together with the inclusion relation. For any <math id="S8.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in Z</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S8.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{A}=\langle X\mid Y_{A}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{A}=\langle X\mid Y_{A}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{A}=\{u_{\psi}=v_{\psi}\mid\psi\in B\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{A}=\{u_{\psi}=v_{\psi}\mid\psi\in B\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S8.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>, then clearly there is a canonical <math id="S8.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid homomorphism <math id="S8.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{A}^{A^{\prime}}:M_{A}\to M_{A^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{A}^{A^{\prime}}:M_{A}\to M_{A^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One checks easily that <math id="S8.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct limit of the direct system <math id="S8.p10.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{M_{A},\alpha_{A}^{A^{\prime}}\mid A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{A},\alpha_{A}^{A^{\prime}}\mid A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of <math id="S8.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now fix an <math id="S8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra obtained from <math id="S8.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by adjoining isomorphisms <math id="S8.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{U_{\psi}}\cong\overline{V_{\psi}}~(\psi\in A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{U_{\psi}}\cong\overline{V_{\psi}}~(\psi\in A)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> are the graded <math id="S8.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules corresponding to <math id="S8.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Then <math id="S8.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R_{A})\cong M_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R_{A})\cong M_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> by Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 7.6. ‣ 7.2. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra I ‣ 7. The graded 𝒱-monoid of a graded Bergman algebra ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>, and this isomorphism maps <math id="S8.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R_{A}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R_{A}]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S8.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>, then clearly there is a canonical graded <math id="S8.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S8.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{A}^{A^{\prime}}:R_{A}\to R_{A^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{A}^{A^{\prime}}:R_{A}\to R_{A^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be the direct limit of the direct system <math id="S8.p12.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{R_{A},\beta_{A}^{A^{\prime}}\mid A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{R_{A},\beta_{A}^{A^{\prime}}\mid A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of graded <math id="S8.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras. Since <math id="S8.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with direct limits, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(\varinjlim R_{A})=\varinjlim\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R_{A})\cong\varinjlim M_{A}=M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>→</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>→</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>→</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(\varinjlim R_{A})=\varinjlim\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R_{A})\cong\varinjlim M_{A}=M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly the isomorphism <math id="S8.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\cong M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\cong M</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S8.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[R]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Theorem 4.10. ‣ 4. Bergman algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>, <math id="S8.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is graded isomorphic to a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra <math id="S8.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> whose grading is induced by a weight map. That <math id="S8.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is hereditary and has a weak universal property follow as in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 8.1. ‣ 8. Realisation of Γ-monoids as non-stable 𝐾-theory of graded rings ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Application to graph algebras</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Graded Classification Conjecture for Leavitt path algebras states that the graded Grothendieck group, <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a complete invariant for
these algebras (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, §7.3.4]</cite>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 9.1</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be finite graphs.</span></p>
<ol id="S9.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any order preserving </span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module homomorphism </span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with
</span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists a unital </span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-graded </span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-homomorphism </span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any order preserving </span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-module
isomorphism </span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with
</span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)]=[L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)]=[L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists a unital </span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-graded </span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isomorphism </span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here the order preserving <math id="S9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphism <math id="S9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))</annotation></semantics></math> should give that these algebras are graded Morita equivalent (see Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm4" title="Conjecture 9.4. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the graded <math id="S9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of Leavitt path algebras coincides with equivariant <math id="S9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of graph <math id="S9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras one can extend the conjecture to the setting of <math id="S9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras.
Denote by <math id="S9.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> the gauge circle actions on <math id="S9.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝕋</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))</annotation></semantics></math> the equivariant <math id="S9.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of <math id="S9.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. There are canonical order preserving isomorphisms of <math id="S9.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-modules (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, p. 275]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Proof of Theorem A]</cite>).</p>
<table id="S9.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L(E))\cong K_{0}(L(E\times\mathbb{Z}))\cong K_{0}(C^{*}(E\times\mathbb{Z}))\cong K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(E))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝕋</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L(E))\cong K_{0}(L(E\times\mathbb{Z}))\cong K_{0}(C^{*}(E\times\mathbb{Z}))\cong K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(E)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus one can pose the analytic version of Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 9.1. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be finite graphs. Then there is an order preserving <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-module
isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))\longrightarrow K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(F))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝕋</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝕋</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))\longrightarrow K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(F)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with
<math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi([C^{*}(E)])=[C^{*}(F)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi([C^{*}(E)])=[C^{*}(F)]</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if
<math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(E)\cong C^{*}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(E)\cong C^{*}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> which respect the gauge action.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In fact in Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 9.1. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a> if the graded Grothendieck groups are isomorphic, one should have that the isomorphism between the Leavitt path algebras is indeed a <math id="S9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math>-isomorphism. If this is the case, then Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 9.1. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a> implies Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm2" title="Conjecture 9.2. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>. For, if <math id="S9.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝕋</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝕋</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(F))</annotation></semantics></math>, then by (<a href="#S9.E1" title="In 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>), <math id="S9.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathbb{C}}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathbb{C}}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathbb{C}}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathbb{C}}(F))</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S9.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{\mathbb{C}}(E)\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}L_{\mathbb{C}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>gr</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbb{C}}(E)\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}L_{\mathbb{C}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> via a <math id="S9.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math>-isomorphism. This implies that <math id="S9.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(E)\cong C^{*}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(E)\cong C^{*}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> which respects the gauge action (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 4.4]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The notion of <em id="S9.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">talented monoids</em> allows us to write the classification conjectures for Leavitt and <math id="S9.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras in a unified manner. The positive cone of the graded Grothendieck group of a Leavitt path algebra <math id="S9.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> can
be described purely based on the underlying graph, via the so-called talented monoid <math id="S9.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. The benefit of talented monoids is that they give us control over elements of the monoids (such as minimal elements, atoms, etc.)
and consequently on the “geometry” of the graphs such as the number of cycles, their lengths, etc. (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_deff">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9.3</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be a row-finite directed graph. The <em id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">talented monoid</em> of <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the commutative
monoid generated by <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{v(i)\mid v\in E^{0},i\in\mathbb{Z}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v(i)\mid v\in E^{0},i\in\mathbb{Z}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to</p>
<table id="S9.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(i)=\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}r(e)(i+1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(i)=\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}r(e)(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for every <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not a sink. The additive group <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> of integers acts on <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> via monoid automorphisms by shifting indices: For each <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{n}v(i)=v(i+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>v</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{n}v(i)=v(i+n)</annotation></semantics></math>, which extends to an action of <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout we will denote elements <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(0)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The crucial ingredient for us is the action of <math id="S9.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> on the monoid <math id="S9.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. The general idea is that the monoid structure of <math id="S9.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> along with the action of <math id="S9.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> resemble the graded ring structure of a Leavitt path algebra <math id="S9.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the conjectures above roughly state that isomorphism of talented monoids can lift to the isomorphisms of graded and equivariant <math id="S9.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory of respected Leavitt and graph <math id="S9.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras. We therefore can formulate both conjectures as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 9.4</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be finite graphs and <math id="S9.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> a field. Then the following are equivalent.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S9.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The talented monoids </span><math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are </span><math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isomorphic;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Leavitt path algebras </span><math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are graded Morita equivalent;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph </span><math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-algebras </span><math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(E)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S9.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are equivariant Morita equivalent.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the following are equivalent.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S9.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The talented monoids </span><math id="S9.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S9.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{F}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are pointed </span><math id="S9.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isomorphic;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Leavitt path algebras </span><math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S9.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are graded isomorphic;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S9.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph </span><math id="S9.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-algebras </span><math id="S9.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(E)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S9.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.I3.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are equivariant isomorophic.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p8.1" class="ltx_p">G. Arnone in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and L. Vas in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> answered part (2) of Conjecture <a href="#S9.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 9.1. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.1</span></a>, independently and with different approaches, in positive. In fact Arnone shows that the lifting map can be diagonal preserving graded <math id="S9.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism and Vas shows that the graphs can consist of infinite edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Using theory developed here, we can in fact show that for any <math id="S9.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S9.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S9.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and any pointed <math id="S9.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism <math id="S9.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can lift <math id="S9.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to a graded <math id="S9.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S9.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>, which preserves the injectivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9.5</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph and let <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. Let <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:T_{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:T_{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid homomorphism with
<math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(1_{E})=[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(1_{E})=[A]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a unital <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(v)\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v)\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S9.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is monomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the semisimple <math id="S9.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S9.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the product of <math id="S9.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E^{0}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E^{0}|</annotation></semantics></math>-copies of the field <math id="S9.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
