<p id="S9.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">for vertices <math id="S9.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> which are not sink. This amounts to adjoining matrices to <math id="S9.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> with relations which define the <math id="S9.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="S9.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> (see Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm15" title="Theorem 5.15. ‣ 5.5. Path algebras as universal algebras ‣ 5. Graded Bergman universal construction ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a>), and thus</span></p>
