<p id="S9.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S9.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. In (<a href="#S9.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.2</span></a>) these relations translate to
<math id="S9.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(i)=\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}r(e)(i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(i)=\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}r(e)(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S9.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the talented monoid <math id="S9.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the graded <math id="S9.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S9.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>, induces
<math id="S9.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)),[p_{v}]\mapsto[L_{K}(E)v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)),[p_{v}]\mapsto[L_{K}(E)v]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose <math id="S9.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:T_{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:T_{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a pointed <math id="S9.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid homomorphism. Since <math id="S9.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(1_{E})=\sum_{v\in E^{0}}\phi(v)=[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(1_{E})=\sum_{v\in E^{0}}\phi(v)=[A]</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a graded <math id="S9.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module isomorphism <math id="S9.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cong\bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}q_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cong\bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}q_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are graded finitely generated <math id="S9.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules with <math id="S9.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(v)=[q_{v}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v)=[q_{v}]</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S9.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}Ae_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}Ae_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are pairwise orthogonal idempotents of homogeneous degree zero in <math id="S9.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S9.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ae_{v}\cong q_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ae_{v}\cong q_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> as graded <math id="S9.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Thus there is a natural graded <math id="S9.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S9.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></msub><mi>𝗄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k}\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>, making the ring <math id="S9.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S9.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S9.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>-ring</mtext><mi>𝗄</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S9.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes_{R}p_{v}\cong q_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes_{R}p_{v}\cong q_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (use <math id="S9.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes_{R}R/I\cong A/AI" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes_{R}R/I\cong A/AI</annotation></semantics></math>, for an ideal <math id="S9.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>), we obtain the following commutative diagram of <math id="S9.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-monoids:</span></p>
