<div id="S9.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, suppose <math id="S9.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(v)\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v)\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S9.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S9.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(v)=[A\otimes_{L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)}L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v]=[A\psi(v)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v)=[A\otimes_{L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)}L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v]=[A\psi(v)]</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S9.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(v)\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(v)\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S9.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the graded uniqueness theorem for Leavitt path algebras (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 2.2.15]</cite>) implies that <math id="S9.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.
∎</span></p>
</div>
