<div id="S9.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 9.6</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be finite graphs and <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))</annotation></semantics></math> a preordered <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-module homomorphism with
<math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a unital <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded homomorphism <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore if <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, so is <math id="S9.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the positive cone of <math id="S9.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>gr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely <math id="S9.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) and <math id="S9.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is preordered, we obtain a <math id="S9.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid homomorphism <math id="S9.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F))</annotation></semantics></math>. The Corollary now follows from Theorem <a href="#S9.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 9.5. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 9.7</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although throughout the paper, by setting the grade group <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to be trivial, we recover the non-graded universal constructions, Corollary <a href="#S9.Thmthm6" title="Corollary 9.6. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.6</span></a>, however, is not valid for the (non-graded) Grothendieck group <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because the positive cone of <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}(L(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}(L(E))</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}(L(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(L(E))</annotation></semantics></math>. As an example, since <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2))=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}(L(2,3))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}(L(2,3))=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have a preordered pointed monoid homomorphism <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2))\rightarrow K_{0}(L(2,3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2))\rightarrow K_{0}(L(2,3))</annotation></semantics></math>, however there does not exist any unital ring homomorphsim <math id="S9.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,3)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,3).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p17.1" class="ltx_p">We finish this section by giving a new proof for the graded
uniqueness theorem for Leavitt path algebras (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 2.2.15]</cite> for an element-wise proof). Although this proof can be extended to arbitrary graphs, for simplicity we work with Leavitt path algebras associated to finite graphs, where all our applications are concerned.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9.8</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph, <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded ring and <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math> a graded ring homomorphism. If <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(v)\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v)\not=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S9.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">The ring homomorphism <math id="S9.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> induces a <math id="S9.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-monoid homomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S9.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S9.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\phi}:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\phi}:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\longrightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>gr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\longrightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S9.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\longmapsto[A\phi(v)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\longmapsto[A\phi(v)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since any graded finitely generated projective <math id="S9.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>-module, is generated by some <math id="S9.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (see the first part of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S9.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 9.5. ‣ 9. Application to graph algebras ‣ Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.5</span></a>) and since <math id="S9.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(v)\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v)\not=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S9.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in E^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in E^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S9.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\phi}^{-1}(0)=\{0\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\phi}^{-1}(0)=\{0\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Suppose <math id="S9.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(a)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S9.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\not=a\in L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\not=a\in L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. Without the loss of generality we can assume <math id="S9.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is homogeneous. Consider the left ideal <math id="S9.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S9.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> is graded von Neumann regular, <math id="S9.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a=L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a=L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)e</annotation></semantics></math>, for some homogeneous idempotent <math id="S9.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S9.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a</annotation></semantics></math> is a graded finitely generated projective module and <math id="S9.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\phi}([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a])=[L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\phi(a)]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝗄</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\phi}([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a])=[L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\phi(a)]=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements</span>
We would like to thank Gene Abrams whose germane questions led us to try to understand Bergman’s paper. Our thanks to George Bergman who provided us with invaluable inputs which improved the presentation of the paper.</p>
</div>
